<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somoza family</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Somoza family ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> as a political dynasty from 1936 to 1979. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a>, head of the US-trained National Guard, seized power in 1936 and, after his assassination in 1956, was succeeded by his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-somoza-debayle">Luis Somoza Debayle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a>. They governed through the Guard and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a>, amassed vast holdings in land and industry, and were overthrown by the Sandinista revolution of 1979.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a>, born in 1896 into a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>-planting family, was educated partly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, where he learned English and met his wife, Salvadora Debayle Sacasa. He joined the Liberal revolt of 1926 and worked as an interpreter in the peace talks with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Under President José María Moncada he served as governor of León and Minister of Foreign Affairs, and when the United States Marines left in 1933 he became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a> they had organised. In 1936 he used the Guard to overthrow President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-sacasa">Juan Bautista Sacasa</a> and was inaugurated on 1 January 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Somoza García held the presidency in 1937–1947 and 1950–1956, at times ruling through puppets, placed relatives and friends in power and backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> foreign policy. He was shot by the poet Rigoberto López Pérez and died on 29 September 1956. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-somoza-debayle">Luis Somoza Debayle</a> restored the ban on immediate re-election in 1959, joined the Central American Common Market in 1960 and took a large part in the failed Bay of Pigs Invasion of 1961. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a>, head of the Guard from 1955 and president in 1967–1972 and 1974–1979, returned to his father's harsher personalist rule.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> earthquake of 23 December 1972, which killed 10,000 people, Somoza Debayle headed the National Emergency Committee and diverted international aid, and the Guard took part in looting. The economic elite began to fund the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandinista-national-liberation-front">Sandinista National Liberation Front</a>. Under a state of siege the Guard engaged in widespread torture, rape, imprisonment and execution of opponents and peasants. The assassination of Pedro Joaquín Chamorro on 10 January 1978 set off mass demonstrations, and the final offensive of the FSLN began in May 1979. Somoza resigned on 17 July 1979 and fled to Miami.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Over four decades the family grew rich through bribes, monopolies, seizures of land and the siphoning of foreign aid. By the 1970s it owned around 23% of the land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, and its wealth has been estimated at about $533 million. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a> went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, where Argentine guerrillas killed him on 17 September 1980.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Somoza family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Somoza family</b> accumulated enormous wealth in land, industry and transport.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Two days after Anastasio Somoza Debayle fled, Sandinista forces entered Managua, ending the <b>Somoza family</b>'s 43-year rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
